PROCESSES, TASK SLOTS, FILE DESCRIPTORS

PicoNIX has a small fixed number of user task slots. A newly run
command, its shell and extra children occupy separate slots. There
is no full Linux process-group/session machinery.

OBSERVE
  ps
  lsof
  lsof PID
  cat /proc/tasks
  cat /proc/memory
  cat /proc/ramfiles
  cat /proc/uptime
  cat /proc/context_switches

A PROCESS SLOT IS NOT A FILE
  Six 24 KiB user slots (platform configuration), each with a
  reserved 4 KiB stack. Code + static data + stack must fit.
  Running interactive or network daemons may exhaust slots.
  Start programs only if slots are available.

SHELL EXECUTION
  hello
  hello &
  echo hello | cat
Background '&' continues after the shell prompt; it does not mean
that a command is harmless or will terminate. Avoid daemons in an
MCP execute_command call unless explicitly planned.

CONTROL
  kill PID        changes process state; requires confirmation
  ps              verify effect
Do not guess PID. Never kill mcpd from the connection you need.
Avoid recursively spawning shells or tracing the shell while slots
are exhausted.

FD DIAGNOSTICS
  lsof [PID]      list open descriptors
  /proc/tasks    task status
FDs are finite (configured per task). Close sockets and files after
use. Blocking read/accept can prevent a command from finishing.
For terminal-sensitive tools (vi, edit, fd, watch, mem, gpio,
trace follow) use an interactive console instead of MCP.
